course: biology
lesson: 395
title: Documentaries vs cartoons: added sounds, edited stories and what is real
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: In a nature film a tiny ant marches across a leaf with loud crunchy footsteps, and an eagle swoops down with a fierce scream. Both sounds were probably added later: the ant was too quiet to hear, and a real bald eagle's call is a weak, high chatter.
explain: Even true documentaries are made, not just recorded. Sound artists add noises, music tells you how to feel, and editors join the best moments, sometimes from several animals, into one story. A good film tells you when it does this.
explain: Cartoons go much further. Tom and Jerry talk, a cartoon bear wears a hat and walks upright, and a cartoon fox raises its eyebrows and smiles, so we can read its feelings like a person's. Real animals show feelings with ears, tails, fur and body shape instead.
explain: The trick is to keep asking: who made this, what was added, and does the animal behave as scientists and books say? Then you can enjoy a cartoon and still know the real animal.
fact: Many sounds in nature films are added later by sound artists, because the camera is often far away and its microphone hears wind rather than the animal.
fact: Sound artists use tricks like squeezing cornflour for footsteps in snow or snapping celery for cracking bones.
fact: The real bald eagle has a weak, high chattering call, so films often use the scream of the red-tailed hawk instead, and so many films do this that most people think eagles really scream that way.
fact: Music in nature films is chosen to make you feel something, but the animals cannot hear it, and the same scene with different music can feel completely different.
fact: Some films join shots of several similar animals to tell the story of one, because it is hard to follow one wild animal everywhere for a year; good films tell you when they do.
fact: Films choose the most exciting moments, so animals seem busier than they really are.
fact: Some scenes are filmed in zoos or wildlife parks, such as polar bear cubs in a den, because filming inside a wild den would harm the animals; people argued whether this should have been said during the show.
fact: Cartoon animals have big eyes, eyebrows and smiles so we can read their feelings, while real animals show feelings with ears, tails, fur and body shape.
fact: Animals communicate with sounds, smells and body movements, but not with words; scientists are studying whale clicks and other sounds with computers, but no animal has been shown to use a language like ours.
fact: Real bears stand on two legs only to look or sniff; they can walk a few steps upright, but it is slow and awkward, and they walk on four legs.
fact: To check a nature video, look for who made it, whether scientists and places are named, and whether the animal behaves as books say; short online clips are sometimes faked, staged or made with computer programs.
fact: Cartoonists often draw four fingers because it is quicker and looks neater, while a real cat has five toes on each front paw and four on each back paw, eighteen in all.
exercise: Why are many sounds in nature films added later? | The real sounds were too quiet or the camera was too far away | Animals refuse to make any sounds at all when they notice cameras close by | Real animal sounds are not allowed on TV | Cameras cannot record any sound at all
exercise: What might a sound artist snap to make the sound of cracking bones? | Celery | A real bone | A glass bottle | A pencil
exercise: What does a real bald eagle's call sound like? | A weak, high chatter | A fierce long scream | A deep roar | A loud honk like a goose
exercise: Whose scream do films often use for an eagle? | The red-tailed hawk's | A hungry lion's | A noisy seagull's | The barn owl's
exercise: Can the animals in a film hear its music? | No, it is added later for the viewers | Yes, it is played to them during filming | Yes, it helps them hunt | Only the birds can hear it
exercise: Why do some films join shots of several animals to tell one animal's story? | It is very hard to follow one wild animal everywhere for a year | Every animal of one kind looks exactly the same, so nobody can tell | It is cheaper to film lots of animals | Animals ask to share the story
exercise: Why do animals in films seem busier than in real life? | Films choose only the most exciting moments | Animals always act faster when they know they are being filmed | Films are played at double speed | Animals in films are trained actors
exercise: Why were some scenes of polar bear cubs filmed in a zoo? | Filming inside a wild den would harm the animals | Polar bears only live in zoos now, there are none left in the wild | Zoo bears are much better actors | Wild dens are too cold for the cameras
exercise: Why do cartoon animals have big eyes, eyebrows and smiles? | So we can read their feelings like people's | Because real animals look exactly like that when you see them up close | Because they can see better that way | To make them look scary
exercise: How do real animals show their feelings? | With ears, tails, fur and body shape | With eyebrows and big smiles, like people do | With words and little songs | They do not show any feelings at all
exercise: How do real cats and mice communicate? | With sounds, smells and body movements | With real words, just like Tom and Jerry do | With little written notes | They never communicate with each other at all
exercise: Has any animal been shown to use a language like ours? | No, not yet | Yes, parrots speak full sentences of their own | Yes, cats talk in their own words | Yes, whales write songs with words
exercise: How do real bears usually walk? | On four legs, standing up only to look or sniff | On two legs all day long, just like the bears in cartoons | On their front legs only | On two legs, but only in winter
exercise: Which is a good clue that a nature video is real? | It names the scientists and places | It has very loud and exciting music | It has an animal that talks | It is very short and very shocking
exercise: How many toes does a real cat have in all? | Eighteen | Sixteen | Twenty | Twelve
exercise: Why do cartoonists often draw four fingers? | It is quicker and looks neater | Real cats have four fingers | It is a rule set by the law | Five fingers are too hard to draw
test: «that ant crunching in the doc, mad how loud ants are» What is most likely true? | The sound was added later; real ant footsteps are far too quiet to hear | Ants really are that loud up close, we just never get near enough to hear them | Ants wear tiny shoes | The microphone was inside the ant
test: In The Lion King the lions talk and sing. Which is true of real lions? | They roar, grunt, use smells and body movements, not words | They talk in words, but only to other lions when people are not near | They sing to their cubs at night | They have no way to communicate at all
test: «Tom's got four fingers, so real cats have 16 toes, right» What is the best correction? | Real cats have 5 toes on each front paw and 4 on each back paw, 18 in all | Right, real cats have 4 toes on every paw, just like Tom, so 16 in all | Real cats have 20 toes, like people | Real cats have no toes, only soft pads
test: Count them: if Tom and the alley cat Butch were real cats, how many toes would they have together? | 36 | 32 | 40 | 18
test: You watch a crocodile scene with silly circus music instead of scary music. What would change? | How you feel about it, though the crocodile does exactly the same | The crocodile would hear the music and start behaving in a sillier way | The crocodile would stop hunting | Nothing, music has no effect on people
test: In a cartoon a bear walks upright in a hat for a whole day. What would a real bear do? | Walk on four legs and stand up only now and then to look or sniff | Walk upright all day long like a person, only without the hat on its head | Hop along on one leg | Crawl on its belly like a snake
test: «the polar bear cubs were in a zoo?? total scam» What is a fair view? | Filming in a zoo can be kinder than disturbing a wild den, but people argue it should be said | Yes, the whole film was invented | No, there are no zoos anywhere in the world that keep polar bears | Zoo animals are not real animals
test: A film follows «one lion» for a whole year. What might really have happened? | Shots of several similar lions may have been joined, and good films say so | One lion wore a camera for a whole year without a single break, even asleep | The lion was a robot | The lion was painted on paper
test: A clip online shows a parrot singing a whole pop song perfectly and chatting about its day. What makes you doubt it? | No named makers or scientists, and it does not fit what parrots are known to do | Parrots cannot make any sounds | All parrot videos online are real, so there is nothing to doubt at all | Parrots only ever sing classical music
test: «real foxes smile when they're happy, cartoons are right» What is the best answer? | Real foxes show feelings with ears, tail and body, and their faces look calm | Foxes smile just like people, with their lips turned up at the corners | Foxes raise their eyebrows | Foxes have no feelings at all
test: A real lion sleeps most of the day. Why might a lion film still look full of action? | The film shows only the most exciting moments | Lions are much busier when they know a camera is filming them | The film is shown backwards | Lions only sleep in zoos
test: Does an added sound make a nature film a lie? | Not if what the animal does is real; good films still show real behaviour | Yes, every film with added sound is fake from beginning to end, so it cannot be trusted | No, because animals make no sounds | Yes, the animal was not there at all
exam: Identify the principal reason for which numerous sounds in nature films are added subsequent to filming. | The genuine sounds were too faint or too distant to be recorded by the camera | The animals declined to produce sounds in the presence of cameras | The broadcasting of genuine animal sounds is prohibited | Cameras are incapable of recording sound under any conditions
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the call of the bald eagle? | It is a weak, high chattering call, frequently replaced in films by the scream of the red-tailed hawk | It is a fierce prolonged scream identical in every respect to that heard in films and broadcasts | It is a deep roar resembling that of a lion | The bald eagle produces no call whatsoever
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning communication among animals. | Animals communicate by sounds, smells and movements, but none has been shown to employ a language comparable to human language | Domestic cats and mice converse in words | Parrots compose original sentences in human languages and employ them to describe past events and future intentions | Animals possess no means of communication
exam: It is given that a domestic cat possesses five toes on each fore paw and four toes on each hind paw. Ascertain the aggregate number of toes. | Eighteen | Sixteen | Twenty | Nine
